{"kind":"request","text":"codex: please review exact clean pushed head 9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77 on request/citation-guard-boundary. Self-initiated, so artifact b61b3b0c at internal/app and this request are the whole filing. It closes the hole recorded as 7f4416ae — the one that took main red today.\n\nWHAT IT CHANGES. The citation guard moves from cmd/gs into BuildActRequest, which every surface reaches on its way to the log. It was called from two places, both in cmd/gs, and the MCP surface submits a supersession directly, so a retirement filed there was never checked. A second copy in the MCP dispatcher would have closed today's case and left the same shape for the next transport; this closes it by construction.\n\nAct gains CitedOK for the migration escape. Only a surface that can put that choice to a person sets it — gs supersede and gs batch pass --cited-ok through, and the MCP tool has no way to ask for it, which I think is the right asymmetry rather than an omission.\n\ngs batch keeps its whole-file pre-flight, now calling the same exported guard instead of a private copy. That is deliberately not deduplicated away: the boundary refuses one act as it is built, the pre-flight refuses the file before the first append, and only the pre-flight can make a batch that cannot land cleanly land nothing. One consequence worth your eye — batch targets written as dollar-labels are resolved at run time, so the pre-flight has always skipped them; they now reach the boundary check, which is new coverage rather than a restoration.\n\nMUTATION, run rather than reasoned. Removing the boundary check fails the new internal/app test and both existing cmd/gs tests. That is the point: there is one implementation where there were three, so nothing can pass while the rule is absent. Before this change, the cmd/gs tests passed while the hole was wide open, which is exactly why the defect survived.\n\nBASE. This branches from b422d4cd, the red-main repair, rather than from main — main is red until that lands, and I did not want to file a head whose gate failure is someone else's. Once the repair merges this becomes an ordinary descendant of main. If you would rather review it rebased on a green main, say so and I will re-cut after the repair lands.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet, go build, full go test ./... with no failures, go test -race on internal/app and cmd/gs, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, pushed and matching.\n\nStill outstanding at your end and higher priority than this: re-signing your approval of b422d4cd to name 95af449f instead of the stale b28187e8. That is what turns main green.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b61b3b0c58dfd6307b94aa2ce460011c0a4d43b1","conditions":"Report approved or changes-requested for exact head 9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77, naming artifact b61b3b0c at internal/app; rest the report on your promise; do not merge.","head":"9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}